# Build output. Every generated asset lands under sites/<name>/build/: the Vite
# bundle, the blog's post PDFs and social cards, analytics' topojson.
#
# It sits inside the site rather than at the repo root because //go:embed cannot
# reference a path above its own package directory, and a release build embeds
# all of it.
build/
bin/
node_modules/

# Generated by `bun run images` from the committed sources in frontend/images/.
# 22 variants that would otherwise churn the repo every time a quality setting
# moved.
/sites/*/frontend/public/images/

# `go build .` inside a site directory writes an executable named after that
# directory, which is how a 14MB binary once got committed. `make build` uses
# -o bin/, but the bare command is the one people type.
/sites/*/isaacbythewood.com
/sites/*/*.com
/sites/*/*.me
!/sites/*.com/
!/sites/*.me/

# The same trap from the repo root: `go build ./sites/<name>/` writes the
# executable into the working directory rather than next to its source, so the
# rules above do not catch it.
/*.com
/*.me

# Same rule for the resume: resume/content.yml and resume/resume.typ are the
# source, this is the output.
/sites/*/frontend/public/pdfs/
/sites/*/frontend/public/og/

# Playwright scratch
.playwright-cli/

# Tunnel credentials never touch the repo: they live in the named volume that
# setup-tunnel.sh creates.
credentials.json
cert.pem
*.pem
.env

# Runtime state: the SQLite database, and the 131MB GeoIP mmdb the server
# downloads on boot. In production both live in a named Docker volume.
/sites/*/data/

# Opt-in local port publishing for eyeballing a site before its DNS exists.
# Machine-specific, never part of a deploy.
docker-compose.local.yml
